# name: (topics, when, skeleton, [(qid, own-words marking description)])
P=[
('Percentages: amount, change and reverse',['N09'],'A percentage of a quantity feeds a longer chain (people, cups, grams, or profit).',
 '1 Write the percentage as a multiplier. 2 Apply it to the starting amount. 3 Carry the result through each later step, labelling every number. 4 Give the final answer with units and, if asked, state the assumption in words.',
 [('jun2023-3f-21','Three process marks: working with 68% (at any stage), any other correct process step (such as people times 2 cups, or grams per person), and the full process to the total grams; an accuracy mark for an answer from 11532.5 to 11533 (full marks if a correct value in range is shown then wrongly rounded); a communication mark for a correct statement about the effect of the assumption (for example that more coffee would be needed).'),
  ('nov2024-3f-21','Process marks in order: start working with the ratio; a complete process for the number of pens and pencils (or for the cost or selling amount of one item); a process for the total cost or total amount sold (or the profit) for both; a complete process for the profit as a percentage or decimal; accuracy for 45.6.')]),
('Fractions and mixed numbers',['N07'],'Add, subtract, multiply or divide fractions, usually with a "show that" or "give as a mixed number" part.',
 '1 Common denominator (or improper fractions). 2 Combine numerators. 3 Convert to a mixed number if asked. 4 For "show that", write each intermediate step and finish on the exact printed answer.',
 [('nov2024-1f-20','Part (a): a method mark for a common denominator with at least one correct numerator, then an accuracy mark for the mixed number. Part (b): method for improper fractions, a dependent method for the division step, and a communication mark that needs a visible intermediate step.'),
  ('nov2022-1f-20','Part (a): method mark for a correct common denominator and one right numerator, accuracy for the mixed number; a special-case mark exists for a decimal equivalent. Part (b): method for the reciprocal multiplication set-up, accuracy for an unsimplified fraction that leads to the target.')]),
('Ratio sharing and recipes',['R01','R03'],'Divide a total in a ratio, or scale a recipe up or down, often feeding a second step.',
 '1 Add the ratio parts. 2 Find one part. 3 Multiply for each share. 4 Use the shares in the next step. 5 Label each quantity.',
 [('jun2019-1f-23','Process marks for starting the scaling (scale factor or the multiple of the base amount), then the complete scaling process, accuracy for the amount; the later part gives a process mark for the butter amount and an accuracy mark that must not come from wrong working.'),
  ('jun2022-1f-25','Process marks for the ratio split, for the number of one type, for a correct first step on a fraction or percentage of that group, for combining the shares, then accuracy; the scheme says a right answer with no working gets no marks.')]),
('Speed, distance and time',['R04'],'Average speed or distance from times in minutes and hours.',
 '1 Convert minutes to hours first. 2 Pick the formula (distance = speed x time, or speed = distance / time). 3 Substitute with units. 4 Combine legs if there are several. 5 State the final speed with units.',
 [('jun2022-3f-27','Process marks for one distance from speed and time, for adding the two distances, for dividing by the right time with consistent units, then accuracy.'),
  ('nov2019-3f-27','A B1 for the time difference (1.18 is allowed, 118 scores B0), a process mark for converting minutes to hours, a process mark for distance divided by the time difference, then accuracy; a special case B2 exists for 83.33 seen as the answer.')]),
('Units, currency and best-value comparisons',['N01','N13'],'Compare two offers, or convert between metric/imperial units or currencies, and finish with a decision.',
 '1 Put both options in the same unit or currency. 2 Work out a unit price or a cost for the same amount. 3 Compare the two numbers side by side. 4 Write the decision in a sentence that names both values.',
 [('jun2022-2f-27','Process marks for a fuel-unit conversion, a currency conversion, and a unit price or proportional step; a communication mark for "No" with comparative figures (rounded or truncated allowed).'),
  ('jun2025-3f-25','Process marks for a mass conversion, a currency conversion, and for comparable figures; unit mix-ups are condoned on those three marks; a communication mark for naming the shop with accurate comparable figures.'),
  ('jun2022-3f-25','Process marks for the cost of a quantity at one shop, for the first step of the discount at the other shop, and for the full discounted cost; a communication mark for naming Chic Decor with fully correct figures.')]),
('Solve linear equations',['A04'],'A worded set-up (counters, books, shelves) whose total gives a linear equation.',
 '1 Write each quantity as an expression in x. 2 Add them and set equal to the total. 3 Collect and solve. 4 Substitute back to find what the question asks for (not always x). 5 Check against the total.',
 [('jun2022-3f-24','Process marks for three expressions (two correct is enough), for summing them to the total, for starting to solve, and for using the solution in the ratio; a final accuracy mark. Trial values that meet the conditions are also credited in the first two steps.'),
  ('nov2023-1f-24','Process marks for setting up an equation in x, for substituting the solved value into an expression, for finding the mass of one book, then for the mass of the books on shelf A; accuracy for the final value.')]),
('Simplify, expand and factorise',['A01','A02'],'Index laws, collecting terms, brackets, and taking out common factors, usually as short lettered parts.',
 '1 Index laws: add powers when multiplying, multiply them for a power of a power. 2 Expand each bracket fully, then collect. 3 For factorising take out the highest common factor (number and letters). 4 Expand back to check.',
 [('jun2022-2f-21','A B1 for the power of a power; a method mark for expanding one bracket or collecting like terms, then accuracy; a method mark for any partial factorisation then accuracy for full factorisation.'),
  ('nov2019-3f-22','Expanding two brackets: method for 3 of the 4 terms right (or all four with signs wrong), then accuracy. Factorising: two marks for the full factor, one for a partial factor.'),
  ('nov2020-3f-23','A B1 for the index result; a method mark for partial simplification, then accuracy; for the inequality, a method mark that needs the working carried out (not only intended), then accuracy.')]),
('Forming formulae and expressions from a context',['A03','A05'],'Write a formula or expression for a perimeter, age or cost, then simplify.',
 '1 Name each unknown. 2 Write each quantity as an expression. 3 Add or combine. 4 Simplify and state the formula in the form asked.',
 [('nov2019-2f-18','Two method marks for building the expression from the given relationships (the second can be earned from two of three correct), accuracy for the final formula; the later simplification part carries one B1.')]),
('Sequences and nth term',['A12'],'Continue a sequence, find a term or the nth term, or check a rule.',
 '1 Find the common difference or the rule. 2 Write the first terms to test it. 3 For nth term: difference x n, then adjust. 4 Check by substituting n = 1 and 2.',
 [('nov2019-2f-26','Method for finding at least two terms of each sequence, method for at least three terms of each, accuracy for the required terms of both.'),
  ('nov2021-3f-25','Process marks for finding the fifth term from the rule, and for forming and using the equation with the total; accuracy for the value.')]),
('Coordinates, gradients and line graphs',['A09','A10','A11'],'Coordinates on a line segment (ratio), reading or writing a straight-line equation, completing tables.',
 '1 For a ratio on a segment: differences in x and y, split, add to the start. 2 For an equation: gradient first, then intercept. 3 Write y = mx + c.',
 [('jun2022-2f-25','Process marks for the coordinate differences, for the ratio step, for one finished coordinate; accuracy for both.'),
  ('jun2024-1f-25','Method mark for the gradient from two points (just circling a value is not enough), second method mark for starting the equation with their gradient or a correct point form, accuracy for a correct equation in any equivalent form, and a separate B1 for a second line with gradient 5 and a non-zero intercept.')]),
('Angle facts and reasons',['G01'],'Parallel lines, polygons, and angles in a figure with reasons given in words.',
 '1 Mark each angle on the diagram. 2 Write the fact (alternate, co-interior, angles on a line, sum of interior angles). 3 Calculate. 4 State the reason beside each step.',
 [('jun2023-3f-22','Method marks for a first and a second angle using parallel-line facts (angles must be labelled or identified), accuracy for both angles, and two communication marks: one parallel-lines reason, and one other correct reason, each linked to their method.'),
  ('jun2019-3f-28','Three method marks: an interior-angle sum (hexagon, or pentagon with the halving shown) or an angle such as AFC or BCF found; using the 2 : 1 ratio correctly (the scheme sets minimum sizes for the angle sums used); and finding angle FED or CDE (81) or a complete process to AFE. Then a communication mark for accurate working leading to AFE = 162 (answer on the diagram needs working and must not be contradicted; 162 without working gets 0).')]),
('Perimeter and area, compound shapes',['G02'],'Compound shapes, circles and fractions of circles, often with a cost or cover question.',
 '1 Split into simple shapes. 2 Area of each. 3 Add or subtract. 4 Convert to the quantity asked (bags, tins). 5 Round up if the answer is a whole number of items.',
 [('jun2024-1f-22','Process marks for the area of one shape, for the total area, and for the area covered by three tins or the litres or tins needed; accuracy for "Yes" with correct figures; a communication mark in part (b) for "no effect" with a reason, which needs a decision in part (a).'),
  ('jun2025-2f-22','Process marks for a relevant area, for the shaded area, and for the number of bags from an area (a length does not count); accuracy; a communication mark for a valid statement about the effect on the number of bags, with a figure-supported "no change" following through.')]),
('Volume and surface area',['G04'],'Cuboids, cylinders, cubes and cover or paint questions.',
 '1 Pick the formula. 2 Work out each face or the volume. 3 Add. 4 Use the extra information (coverage, side length). 5 Give units and compare if asked.',
 [('jun2019-3f-29','Process marks for the area of a circle, for the curved surface area, and for using the coverage per tin with an area (a volume voids the mark), a dependent process mark for the total area or tins for three tanks, and a communication mark for "No" with accurate figures.'),
  ('nov2019-2f-29','Process marks for adding three or more face areas, for the full surface area of the cuboid, for the cube side length from the surface area (square root), and a dependent mark for the volume or surface area of the cube; accuracy for "No" with matching figures.')]),
('Pythagoras and trigonometry',['G05','G06'],'Right-angled triangles: a missing side, a perimeter, or an angle.',
 '1 Sketch the triangle and label sides. 2 Pythagoras for two known sides, or choose sin, cos, tan by the sides you have. 3 Substitute. 4 Solve. 5 Round only at the end.',
 [('nov2021-2f-25','Process marks for squaring and adding, for the hypotenuse, and for the full perimeter (dependent on the earlier one); accuracy for a value in a range.'),
  ('jun2019-2f-24','A method mark for the sine statement and an accuracy mark for a value in a range.')]),
('Transformations',['G11','G12'],'Reflection, rotation, enlargement, translation, and equivalent descriptions.',
 '1 Name the transformation. 2 Give the full details (mirror line, centre and angle, scale factor and centre, vector). 3 Draw with a check on one vertex.',
 [('nov2022-1f-11','Four separate B marks, one each for the scale factor, the centre, the mirror line drawn and its equation.'),
  ('jun2019-1f-26','A method mark for the reflection shown on the diagram, then accuracy marks for each value.')]),
('Probability: tables and unknown probabilities',['P01','P02'],'Probabilities that sum to 1, then a count from a given frequency.',
 '1 Add the known probabilities. 2 Subtract from 1. 3 Share the rest as the question says. 4 To get a total, divide a known frequency by its probability.',
 [('jun2025-1f-20','Process marks for subtracting from 1 and for sharing the remainder, accuracy; a method mark for the total count, accuracy.'),
  ('jun2019-1f-22','Process mark for the sum of unknowns, accuracy; process mark for the total from frequency divided by probability, accuracy.')]),
('Averages and the range',['S01'],'Mean from a list or frequency table, a missing value, or a comparison of two lists.',
 'Mean: 1 Add all the values (or multiply each value by its frequency and add). 2 Divide by the count (or total frequency). 3 For a missing value, total = mean x count, then subtract the known values. Median: 1 Put the values in order. 2 Take the middle one, or the mean of the middle two. 3 For a frequency table, find the position (n+1)/2 first. Mode: the most common value, or the class with the highest frequency. Range: largest minus smallest (a single number). For a comparison write one fact about an average and one about the spread.',
 [('jun2025-2f-25','Process marks for the mean of list A, for starting the ratio, for completing the ratio work, and for working with the mean of list B or removing brackets and the denominator; accuracy for the missing value.'),
  ('nov2023-1f-21','Process marks for the total of the five sticks and for the missing mean, accuracy, and a communication mark for an explanation.')]),
('Charts and scatter graphs',['S02','S03'],'Plot or read a scatter graph, describe correlation, line of best fit, and judge an outlier.',
 '1 Plot or read. 2 Draw a line of best fit through the middle of the trend. 3 Read across and down. 4 Describe the correlation in words, and say whether a point fits the trend.',
 [('nov2021-3f-21','A B1 for the plotted point, method for reading from the line of best fit, accuracy for an answer in a range, communication for the decision about the point.'),
  ('nov2022-2f-18','A B1 for a plotted point, a communication mark for a correct description (negative correlation accepted), a method mark for a line of best fit or a horizontal read-across at 7, and accuracy for an answer in a range.')]),
('Maps, scale and bearings',['R08'],'Scale drawings, map distances and unit conversion.',
 '1 Measure carefully. 2 Multiply or divide by the scale. 3 Convert units (cm to km, m to cm). 4 Check the answer is sensible.',
 [('nov2021-3f-12','Method for measured length times scale (a range is allowed), accuracy in a range; method for a unit conversion and accuracy.'),
  ('nov2019-2f-15','Method for converting metres to cm, method for using the scale, accuracy.')]),
('Venn diagrams and set notation',['P03'],'Fill a Venn diagram, then find a probability from it.',
 '1 Fill the overlap first. 2 Then each set only. 3 Then outside. 4 Check the total. 5 Probability = chosen region / total.',
 [('jun2019-3f-24','Two method marks for filling regions (overlap; then single-set and outside regions), a communication mark for all numbers placed, a method mark for identifying the numerator or denominator of the probability (follow-through from the diagram allowed), accuracy for the probability as a fraction, decimal or percentage.'),
  ('nov2020-2f-22','Method marks for correct numbers in at least one, then two regions, accuracy for all regions, a method mark for a probability fraction (follow-through from the diagram), and accuracy for a fraction, decimal or percentage.')]),
('Rounding, estimation and standard form',['N06','N10','N11'],'Compare values after rounding, estimate, or decide using standard form.',
 '1 Round each value to 1 significant figure. 2 Calculate with the rounded values. 3 Compare with the exact. 4 Say whether it is an over- or under-estimate and why.',
 [('jun2022-2f-24','Process marks for the area of one site, for the area per person at one site, and at both; accuracy for an answer in a range; a communication mark for a valid statement about how square units scale (the scheme lists acceptable and non-acceptable wording).'),
  ('jun2024-1f-24','Process mark for using values rounded to 1 significant figure, a process mark for the full process to the total amount, accuracy following through their rounded values, and a communication mark that must relate to estimation (over- or under-estimate), following through from part (a).')]),
('Inequalities and graph solutions',['A07','A08'],'Solve an inequality, show it on a number line, or read roots from a graph.',
 '1 Treat like an equation. 2 Flip the sign only if you multiply or divide by a negative. 3 Open circle for < or >, closed for <= or >=. 4 Read graph roots at y = 0.',
 [('jun2019-2f-20','Method mark for isolating the terms in n (the sign is ignored for this mark), accuracy for the inequality; method mark for the step on the double inequality; method mark for the line on the number line (circle conventions listed in the scheme); accuracy for the final representation.'),
  ('nov2020-3f-28','A B1 for the first answers (-2 and 4); a method mark for a correct method such as marking the x-axis intercepts, or one correct answer (coordinate answers need both coordinates for the mark); accuracy for both values inside the stated ranges (about 0.55 to 0.65 and 3.35 to 3.45), with no extras.')]),
]
